erated the data i mean let's  say for example of um ngs sequencing  right so so you get all all the data  coming out from your illumina or  nanopore or whatever sequencing  machine and then you try and analyze  this data but then you will also have to  understand how the data has been  generated what was the objective of it  what was the outcome we are trying to  achieve so there's basically a lot of  interactions and collaborations happens  among us but having said that there is  definitely a possibility a one team can  sit remotely and can you know work on  the the coding part of it as long as  they they can effectively communicate  with the scientist you know remotely  it's absolutely possible to to deliver  even even the bioinformatics projects as  well  perfect perfect i think that that kind  of answers the question that  once wanted to ask because what is  happening is that like we saw that that  many especially in the context of women  who has  maybe dropped out and have a long gap  they're coming back and saying that  bioinformatics is something that could  be done effectively in a home setting  and maybe in a hybrid setting so make  sense thank you so much nali for that  for that expertise expert advice  now uh a question i mean uh  for dr khan as he was talking about  networking so there's a question by one  of the student and it makes sense like  after you network with the expert in the  field  what tips do you have on sustaining that  professional relationship for a long  time of approach because we do start  with that but how to sustain that like  how do you find those you know ways to  make keep it engaging engaged and give  that engagement for a longer time or  longer  timeline  yes that's a very good question so  during the conferences or webinars we  make the initial meeting and greeting  right but how do you continue that  relationship outside the webinar uh so  the best place is linkedin obviously  linkedin on twitter so twitter  uh  i was not usi